# Template. Copy to config/op-refs.env (gitignored) and fill in your 1Password
# refs:  cp config/op-refs.env.example config/op-refs.env
#
# op-refs.env maps ENV_VAR = op://vault/item/field. When the gateway spawns a
# host MCP server it resolves those refs from 1Password and injects them as env
# vars — the secret never touches disk or the sandbox. A server with no creds
# (pio) needs no entry.
#
# This file holds op://vault/item/field REFERENCES only, plus the documented
# non-secret env allowlist (GOG_ACCOUNT, GOG_HOME, GOG_KEYRING_BACKEND).
# Everything secret (tokens, keyring passwords) is an op:// ref resolved from
# 1Password at spawn time — never a pasted secret.
#
# Every line below is COMMENTED OUT: a freshly-copied file has zero active
# entries. Uncomment + fill in a line only when you wire that server.
#
# Verify:  op read "op://<vault>/<item>/<field>" >/dev/null && echo OK
# Tip:     1Password app -> right-click a field -> "Copy Secret Reference" gives the
#          exact ref (and disambiguates duplicate item names by ID).

# slack MCP server (its bot/user token). Required to register slack.
# SLACK_TOKEN=op://<vault>/<item>/<field>

# gog (Google Workspace) MCP server. The gateway spawns `gog … mcp`
# non-interactively on the host, so gog must NOT prompt for a keyring password:
# point it at a file-backed keyring and supply the password from 1Password.
# Uncomment + fill in to enable gog (also set GOG_ACCOUNT + MCP=gog in local.mk).
# GOG_ACCOUNT=you@example.com
# GOG_HOME=$HOME/.config/gog
# GOG_KEYRING_BACKEND=file
# GOG_KEYRING_PASSWORD=op://<vault>/<item>/<field>

# Private overlay servers add their own refs here (kept out of the public repo).
